@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Simple Yourdon-style Data Flow Diagram. One process, one data
'                 store, one external entity, with labeled data-flow arrows.
'   usecase:      Teaching the three core DFD shapes (process=circle, data
'                 store=open rectangle, external entity=square), labeling data
'                 flows, and the concept of leveling (context vs exploded).
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       e-commerce / order processing
' ============================================================================

title Data Flow Diagram - Simple Order (Yourdon)

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' A Data Flow Diagram (DFD) shows how DATA moves through a system — not control
' flow, not timing, not sequence. Just: source -> process -> store -> sink.
'
' Yourdon / DeMarco notation (used here):
'   Process         = circle (bubble) with a number.       usecase shape
'   Data Store      = open-ended rectangle (two horizontal lines).  storage
'   External Entity = square (source/sink outside the system).      rectangle
'
' Rules:
'   - Every flow arrow is LABELED with the data package moving (noun).
'   - A process must have at least one inflow AND one outflow.
'   - External entities are OUTSIDE the system boundary; stores are INSIDE.
'   - Flows connect entity<->process, process<->process, process<->store.
'     NEVER entity<->entity or store<->store directly.
'
' LEVELING: a DFD is drawn at a LEVEL. Level 0 (context) shows the whole system
' as ONE process. Level 1 EXPLODES that process into sub-processes. Each level
' must be INPUT/OUTPUT BALANCED with the one above (same flows cross the boundary).
' This simple example is effectively a context diagram for order processing.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' === External entity (square) ===
' The CUSTOMER is outside the system — a source of orders and a sink of receipts.
rectangle "Customer" as Customer <<externalEntity>>

' === Process (circle/bubble) ===
usecase "1\nProcess\nOrder" as P1 <<process>>

' === Data store (open-ended rectangle) ===
storage "Orders\n(D1)" as D1 <<dataStore>>

' === Labeled data flows ===
' Customer sends an order into the process.
Customer --> P1 : order

' Process writes the order to the store.
P1 --> D1 : order record

' Process sends a confirmation back to the customer (sink).
P1 --> Customer : confirmation

note bottom of P1
  **DFD element legend (Yourdon)**
  circle  = Process (number it: 1, 2, 1.1, 1.2 ...)
  storage = Data Store (number it: D1, D2 ...)
  square  = External Entity (source/sink)
  --
  Rule: arrows carry DATA (nouns), not control.
  Rule: no direct entity-to-entity or store-to-store flows.
  --
  Leveling: this is the CONTEXT level. Exploding process "1"
  into 1.1, 1.2 ... yields a Level-1 DFD with the SAME external
  flows crossing the boundary (input/output balance).
end note
@enduml
